# Oxford Ch1 Signs and Symptoms of Psychiatric Disorders

## Introduction

## General Issues

- **Psychopathology** - by definition <u>abnormal states of mind</u>
- **Approaches to psychopathology** - two distinct approaches to the subject:
  - **Descriptive psychopathology** - objective description of abnormal states of mind avoiding, as far as possible, pre-conceived ideas or theories, and limited to the description of conscious experiences and observable behaviour:
    - Brought to popularity by <u>Karl Jaspers</u> classic work <u>General psychopathology</u>
    - Aimed not just in description of symptomatology, but to <u>elucidate the essential qualities of morbid mental experiences</u>, through understanding patient's experience of illness
    - Subsequently these qualities should be elicited, identified, or inferred from structural psychiatric assessment
  - **Experimental psychopathology** - dogmatic approach in describing, and subsequently explaining morbid mental experiences:
    - Initially popularised by <u>Sigmund Freud</u>'s psychoanalytic investigations, which diverged into various psychoanalytic schools of thought
    - Subsequently more empirically measurable and verifiable conscious psychological processes are revealed by cognitive and behavioural psychology
- **Symptoms and signs in psychiatry** - difference is less clear cut as in general medicine and is used more interchangeably:
  - In fact, most of what the patient describes can be symptoms
  - Signs are interpretations by the observer-clinician that is not spoken by the patient, or that is spoken by the patient but can be interpreteted as a sign of a psychiatric illness
- **Subjectivity and objectivity:**
  - <u>Objective</u> - features observed during an interview, e.g. the patient's apperanace and behaviour, and usually used as comparison w/ patient description of Sx (e.g. poor eye contact and psychomotor retardation in relation to described low mood)
  - <u>Subjective</u> - features that are described by the patient, irrespective whether it is objectively demonstrable during te interview
- **Form and content:**
  - <u>Form</u> - abstracted nature of the morbid mental experience with distinct qualities, which is diagnostically useful
  - <u>Content</u> - diagnostically less useful, but can be severely concerning or distressing to the patient, and is important in management (esp. risk assessment)
- **Primary and secondary** - two different meanings:
  - <u>Temporal relationship</u> - based on which occuring first (does not assume causal relationship, where both symptom can arise from the same pathological process)
  - <u>Causal relationship</u> - the secondary symptom arises as a reaction to the primary symptom
- **Understanding and explanation** - Jaspers contrasted two forms of understanding to symptoms:
  - <u>Understanding</u> (Verstehen) - appreciating of the patient's subjective experience through intuition and empathy (e.g. what does it feel like)
  - <u>Explanation</u> (Erklaren) - accounting for the mental phenomenons in terms of external factors (i.e. psychiatric etiology)
- **Significance of individual symptoms:**
- **The patient's experience:**
  - Psychopathology goes beyond just symptomatology, but also concerned w/ patient's experience of illness (i.e. understanding, not explanation)
  - Particularly how the illness changed his view of self, hopes for future, and views of the world
  - The same "symptomatology" at the same severity can have very different effects on different people based on life experiences
  - Achieved through life-story approaches
- **Cultural variations in psycopathology:**

## Description of Symptoms and Signs

### Disturbances of Emotion and Mood

### Disturbances of Perception

- **Definition and terminology:**
  - <u>Perception</u> - process of becoming aware of what is presented through the sense organs, which can be attended or ignored, but cannot be terminated by an effort or will
  - <u>Imagery</u> - awareness of a percept that has been generated within the mind, which can be called up or terminated by an effort or will
- **Dimensions of perception:**
  - Intensity (e.g. some people are more senitive to noise; in mania, perception is more vivid than usual)
  - Quality
- **Illusions:**
  - By definition <u>miperception of external stimuli</u>
  - There is presence of an external stimuli stimulating a sense organ
  - Occurs when general level of sensory stimulation is reduced (e.g. reduced GC), or when attention is not focused on the relevant sensory modality (e.g. not looking intensely)
- **Hallucinations:**
  - **Definition** - a percept that is experienced in the <u>absence of the external stimuli to the corresponding sense organs</u>, usually indicative of significant psychiatric disorder
  - **Characteristic of hallucinations** (form of hallucinations):
    - Occuring at full consciousness
    - Perceived as experienced from the outside world or from within the person's body (real rather than imagined)
    - Cannot be terminated by will
  - **Pseudohallucinations** - that do not fit all the necessary features of hallucinations:
    - Usually does not have the same implication as hallucinations
    - Usually not seeming to the patient to represent reality, rather occuring in mind rather than external space
    - May be perceived from arising from the external world but unreal
  - **Hallucinations can occur in otherwise normal people:**
    - <u>Verge of consciousness</u> - when falling asleep (hypnagogic hallucinations), or on waking (hypnopompic hallucinations), which occurs frequently in narcolepsy
    - <u>Sensory deprivation</u> - occurs in people w/ blindness, or deafness of peripheral origins
    - <u>Organic brain disease</u> - neurological disorder of the visual pathway, epilepsy, Charles Bonnet syndrome
  - **Classification and description of hallucinations:** 
  ![](../img/description_of_hallucinations.png)
    - **Elementary vs complex hallucinations** - elementary are usually fleeting (e.g. bangs, whistles, or flashes of light), while complex hallucinations are more persistent and vivid (e.g. voices, music, seeing faces and scenes)
    - **Classification based on sensory modality:**
      - <u>Auditory hallucinations</u> - further described based on complexity, w/ additional features, such as second-person AH, third-person AH, Gedankenlautwerden (voices speaking the patient thoughts as one is having them), or echo de la pensee (repeated immediatedly after the thought occur)
      - <u>Visual hallucinations</u> - classified based on complexity, where complex VH may be normal or bizarre (e.g. seeing ghosts); additional features such as extracampine (seeing outside visual field)
      - <u>Olfactory and gustatory hallucinations</u> - often unpleasant, not uncommon and may lead to delusional interpretation (e.g. being drugged)
      - <u>Tatile hallucinations</u> - sensation of being touched, pricked or strangled
      - <u>Autoscopic hallucination</u> - experience of seeing one's own body projected in external spaces (i.e. out-of-body experience)
      - <u>Reflex hallucination</u> - rare phenomenon where stimulation of one sensory modality results in hallucination in another (e.g. smelling music)
  - **Clinical associations of hallucinations:**
    - **Auditory hallucinations** - usually of diagnostic significance:
      - <u>Third person AH</u> - strongly a/w [[Schizophrenia]], often experienced on commenting the patient's intentions or actions or making critical coments
      - <u>Second-person AH</u> - directly addressing the patient (i.e. you), but does not suggest a particular diagnosis, but content may suggest the diagnosis (e.g. derogatory nature suggest severe depression especially if accepted \[consider schizophrenia if resented\])
    - **Visual hallucination** - always suggest possibility of organic disorder, but presence may also occur in severe affective disorders, schizophrenia, and dissociative disorder; content does not usually matter but autoscopic hallucinations raises suspicion of temporal lobe epilepsy)
    - **Olfactory or gustatory hallucinations** - not uncommon in schizophrenia or severe depressive disorders, but also suggestive of organic pathologies like temporal lobe epilepsy, and tumours affecting olfactory bulb or pathway
    - **Somatic hallucinations** - suggestive of schizophrenia especially if bizzarre in interpretation
- **Delusional perception:**
  - A type of delusion that is erroneously considered to be a disorder of perception
  - A delusion arising from a normal percept

### Disturbances of Thoughts

1.  Delusions ([Note not found: Delusions and Other Disorders of the Content of Thought](id:5ba36f4a-c635-4a77-afa0-37930b3da55a))

    - **Definition** - a belief that is firmly held on inadequate grounds, that is not affected by rational argument or evidence to the contrary, and that is not a conventional belief that the person might be expected to hold given their educational, cultural, and religious background
    - **Differentiating between delusions in psychosis and strongly held, but abnormal ideas in healthhy individuals:**
      - Based on the definition, it is to be firmly held on inadequate grounds, and should be inappropriate relative to the background if the individual
      - Several limitations of the definition as shown below: 
      ![](../img/limitations_of_the_definition_of_delusion.png)
    - **Althoough not defined, delusions should:**
      - Have marked effects on person's feeling, and action, in the same way strongly held normal beliefs do
      - It is the strange behaviours enforced by the delusion that appears out of keeping, and that it first brings the person to psychiatric attention
      - However, <u>double orientation</u> (usually in <u>chronic schizophrenia</u>) occurs where the delusions have little influence on behaviour on feeling and actions (e.g. grandiose delusion but contentedly working a low-paying, tedious job)
    - **Classification and descriptions of delusions:** 
    ![](../img/descriptions_of_delusions.png)
    - **Primary vs secondary delusions:**
    - **Causes of primary delusions** - delusions that appear suddenly to delusional intensity w/ full conviction:
      - **The sudden delusional idea** - autochthonous derivation of a delusion for no reason, w/ no prior mental phenomenon, and w/ no prior thoughts of this kind
      - **Delusional mood** (Wahnstimmung) - the change of mood preceeds the delusion (as contrary to the fact that others respond emotionally to the delusion):
        - Usually a <u>sense of foreboding</u>, that some, as yet, unidentified sinister event is about to take place
        - Delusion that follows appears to <u>explain the feeling</u>
      - **Delusional perception** - usually attachment of new significance to a familiar percept w/o any reason to do so:
        - <u>Delusional interpretation</u> of the significance of normal perception (e.g. interpretting receiving mail \[normal daily even\] as death threats)
        - The perception is normal and does not account for hallucination
    - **Other delusional experiences:**
      - <u>Delusional misidentification</u> - misidentification of one-self and other disorders, which may be a symptom, or a syndrome (delusional misidentification disorder)
      - <u>Delusional memory</u> - delusional interpretation of a past event, either as attributing significance to a correctly-recalled past events, or that the memory is incorrectly recalled
      - <u>Shared delusions</u> - folie a deux, as seen in socially isolated individuals
    - **Delusional themes** - usually categorised into main themes for the sake of clinical classification:
      - **Persecutory delusions:**
        - <u>Definition</u> - concerned w/ persons or organiations that are thought to be trying to inflict harm on the patient, damage his reputation, or make him insane
        - <u>Epidemiology</u> - common psychopathology, but can occur in various conditions:
          - Organic states (esp. delirium)
          - Delusional disorders
          - Psychotic disorders and schizophrenia-spectrum disorders
          - Severe affective states
        - <u>Differentiating causes of percusatory delusions</u> - can be differentiated based on the **attitude of patients towards the disorders:**
          - In severe psychotic depression, patients **generally accepts** the supposed activities of persecutors as **justified by own wickedness**
          - In schizophrenia (or manic states), these acttivities are often **resented and perceived to be unwanted**
      - **Referential delusions:**
        - <u>Definition</u> - concerned with the idea that objects, events, or people that are unconnectde with the patient have personal significance for him (not exactly in a persecutory or derogatory way)
        - <u>Rated themes of referential delusions</u>:
          - Persecutory associations
          - Derogatory associations
          - Grandiose associations
          - Reassuring themes
      - **Delusions of jealousy** (delusion of infidelity):
        - <u>Definition</u> - beliefs of the infidelity of the patient
        - <u>Clinical significance</u> - potentially dangerous (see section on morbid jealousy)
      - **Delusions of control** (passivity phenomena):
        - <u>Definition</u> - concerned w/ the fact that actions, impulses, or thoughts are controlled by an outside agency
        - <u>Important implications of delusions of control</u>:
          - Extremely suggestive of schizophrenia
          - Forensic implications, hence must be elicited and documented
        - <u>Differentiation bewteen passivity and other similar phenomenons</u> - the sense of agency as the key defining features:
          - Those w/ delusion of control believes that all movements and thoughts are **controlled firmly** by a non-divine outside agency, and cannot be **willed by himself**
          - This must be differentiated from **obidience towards hallucinatory voices**
          - This is further complicated by religious or metaphysical views on free will
      - **Thought alienation** (delusions concern possession of thought):
        - <u>Definition and terminology</u>:
          - Normal, pre-reflective assumption that thoughts are 1) private, and 2) generated on their own
          - Thought alientation occurs when these pressupositions are lost
        - <u>Association</u> - first rank Sx of schizophrenia
        - <u>Forms of thought alienation</u>:
          - **Thought insertion** - delusion that certain thoughts are not the patient's own but are implanted by outside agency, and is usually a/w an **explanatory delusion** (e.g. radio waves)
          - **Thought withdrawal** - delusion that thoughts have been taken out of the mind, which is accompanied by **thought blocking on MSE**, also accompanied by explanatory delusions
          - **Thought broadcasting** - delsuion that unspoken thoughts are known to others, often explained by radiowaves, telepathy, or some other way
      - **Grandiose delusions:**
        - <u>Definition</u> - beliefs related to inflated self-esteem and exaggerated self-importance
        - <u>Contents of grandiose delusions</u> - often related to:
          - Special skill
          - Special ideas
          - Special connections to endowed individuals
        - <u>Clinical associations</u>:
          - Manic episodes
          - Schizophrenia
      - **Delusions of guilt** (depressive delusions):
        - <u>Definition</u> - excssive guilt towards current morbid experience or prior events
        - <u>Contents of delusion of guilt</u> - variable:
          - Can be specific, such as a minor infringement of law in the distant past will be discovered and bring shame upon the patient
          - Can be more general, where a generalised sinfulness or worthlessness will bring retribution to family and friends
        - <u>Clinical association</u> - severe depressive illness
      - **Nihlistic delusions:**
        - <u>Definition</u> - beliefs that a person, bodily functions (e.g. dead bowel), concepts, or the external world will cease to exist
        - <u>Clinical associations</u> - severe depressive illness
      - **Hypochondriacal delusions:**
        - <u>Definition</u> - beliefs despite medical evidence that one is suffering from a disease
        - <u>Epidemiology</u> - More common in elderly
      - **Sexual or amorous delusions** (erotic delusions):
        - <u>Definition</u> - see section on erotomania
        - <u>Clinical associations</u>:
          - Primary delusional disorders and erotomania
          - Grandiosity or delusions of reference
          - Somatic hallucinations felt in the genitalia
    - **Other descriptors of delusions:**
      - **Bizarre and non-bizarre delusions** - not included in current diagnostic criteria due to problems w/ reliability and definitions:
        - <u>Bizarre delusions</u> - delusions that are of highly impropable ccontent, use of this terminlogy gives weight towards the Dx of schizophrenia
        - <u>Non-bizarre delusions</u> - delusions where content is probable, despite current lack of supporting evidence
      - **Mood-congruent and mood-incongruent delusions:**
        - <u>Mood-congruent delusions</u> - delusions that make sense in terms of persons current mood (e.g. nihlistic/ hypochondriacal delusions in depression; grandiose delusions in mania)
        - <u>Mood-incongruent delusions</u> - delusions out of keeping of prevailing mood which is suggestive of psychotic disorders and schizophrenia

